Let $L$ be even, $b_j$ real, and $0<\lambda<\pi$. A real solution of <a href="#eq:consecutive-counting" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:consecutive-counting">[eq:consecutive-counting]</a> satisfies <a href="#eq:bethe-equations" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:bethe-equations">[eq:bethe-equations]</a>. If its Bethe vector is nonzero, then $|Y\rangle$ is an eigenvector of $e^{it}\mathsf A(x)+e^{-it}\mathsf L(x)$ with eigenvalue $\tau_t(x;Y)$, and $\mathcal P_L|Y\rangle$ is an eigenvector of the physical twisted row with eigenvalue $(-1)^n\tau_t(x;Y)$. The Jacobian of <a href="#eq:consecutive-counting" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:consecutive-counting">[eq:consecutive-counting]</a> is $$\label{eq:Gaudin-matrix}
 G_{jk}=\xi_Y'(y_j)\delta_{jk}+K(y_j-y_k),\qquad
 \xi_Y'(u)=\sum_{a=0}^{L-1}s(u-b_a)-\sum_{k=1}^nK(u-y_k).$$ If $\det G\ne0$, the roots have a real analytic continuation for real $t$ near the given value, and on that branch $$\label{eq:twist-root-derivative}
 G\frac{\,\mathrm dY}{\,\mathrm dt}=\frac1\pi\mathbf 1.$$ For $\lambda\ge\pi/2$, $G$ is positive definite at every real root list. Whenever $\xi_Y'(y_j)>0$, write $$\label{eq:Gaudin-weights}
 \ell_j=\frac1{\xi_Y'(y_j)},\qquad
 (K\ell)_{jk}=K(y_j-y_k)\ell_k;
 \qquad G\mathop{\mathrm{diag}}(\ell)=I+K\ell.$$ These assertions concern finite matrices; they give no uniform bound on $(I+K\ell)^{-1}$ as $L$ increases.

In the normalization <a href="#eq:standard-monodromy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:standard-monodromy">[eq:standard-monodromy]</a>, $$\label{eq:Slavnov-exact}
 S(Y,U)=e^{-int}\left(\prod_{y\in Y}l(y)\right)
 \frac{\det_{j,k}\partial_{y_j}\tau_t(U_k;Y)}
      {\det C(Y,U)}.$$ Consequently, if $\det G_Y\ne0$, then $S(Y,Y)\ne0$ and $$\label{eq:Gaudin-norm-exact}
 S(Y,Y)=(2\pi i s_\lambda)^n
       \left(\prod_{y\in Y}a(y)l(y)\right)
       \left(\prod_{j\ne k}f(y_j-y_k)\right)\det G_Y.$$ Suppose $U$ is obtained by replacing the roots indexed by $H=(p_1,\ldots,p_k)$ by $V=(v'_1,\ldots,v'_k)$, in those same slots. Set $$A_Y(p)=a(p)\prod_{y\in Y\setminus\{p\}}f(y-p)\quad(p\in Y).$$ Then the normalized scalar product is exactly $$\label{eq:deleted-scalar-product}
 \frac{S(Y,U)}{S(Y,Y)}
 =(-s_\lambda)^{-k}
   \frac{\prod_{v'\in V}a(v')F_Y(v')}{\prod_{p\in H}A_Y(p)}
   \frac{\det\bigl((G_Y^{-1}m_{v'}^t)_p\bigr)_{H,V}}
        {\det\bigl(1/[v'-p]\bigr)_{H,V}}.$$ If the positive weights <a href="#eq:Gaudin-weights" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:Gaudin-weights">[eq:Gaudin-weights]</a> are defined, the last numerator equals $$\label{eq:W-determinant}
 \det\bigl((G_Y^{-1}m_{v'}^t)_p\bigr)_{H,V}
 =\left(\prod_{p\in H}\ell_p\right)
       \det\bigl(W_{v'}(p)\bigr)_{H,V},
 \qquad W_{v'}=(I+K\ell)^{-1}m_{v'}^t.$$ Empty products and determinants have value one.

For even $L$ and real inhomogeneities, $$\label{eq:B-Hilbert-adjoint}
 \mathsf B(x)^*=-\mathsf C(\bar x).$$ Thus for a distinct real on-shell list with $\det G_Y\ne0$, the physical unit vector $$\label{eq:physical-Bethe-vector}
 \Omega_Y=\frac{\mathcal P_L|Y\rangle}{\|\mathcal P_L|Y\rangle\|}$$ is well defined, and $$\|\mathcal P_L|Y\rangle\|^2=(-1)^nS(Y,Y)>0.$$ If $Y,Z$ are two such real on-shell lists of the same size, possibly for different twists, then $$\label{eq:two-orientation-overlap}
 |\langle\Omega_Y,\Omega_Z\rangle|^2
       =\frac{S(Y,Z)S(Z,Y)}{S(Y,Y)S(Z,Z)}.$$ These identities also hold for real staggered $b_j$, without a Perron interpretation of the vectors.

Expand each auxiliary mark as $$T^{D_j}=\sum_{\alpha,\beta}(D_j)_{\beta\alpha}
 M_{{\rm ph},\alpha\beta}.$$ For each resulting entry word, let $n_j$ be the number of minus spins after its $j$th action, with $n_0=n$. Entry words with $n_m\ne n$ have zero expectation in $\Omega_Y$. For a path of a word with $n_m=n$, let $H\subset Y$ be the roots absent at the end and let $V$ be its surviving added arguments, ordered in the same replacement slots. Then $|H|=|V|=:k\le m$, and its contribution to the normalized physical expectation is $$\label{eq:exact-path-determinant}
 \left(\prod_{j=1}^m(D_j)_{\beta_j\alpha_j}\right)
 (-1)^{\sum_{j=1}^m(n_{j-1}-n)}
 (-s_\lambda)^{-k}
 \frac{\prod_{j=1}^m\gamma_j}
      {\prod_{j=1}^m(1+q_Y(v'_j))}
 \frac{\det(W_{v'}(p))_{H,V}}
      {\det(1/[v'-p])_{H,V}}
 \prod_{p\in H}\ell_p.$$ The expectation is the sum of these contributions. In particular, the determinant size is bounded by the word length. For diagonal entry words the parity sign in <a href="#eq:exact-path-determinant" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:exact-path-determinant">[eq:exact-path-determinant]</a> is one. Among distinct removed original roots the correction product contains only reciprocal $f$ factors, with one for each pair. Each removed original root also contributes one factor $\beta$ joining it to the row argument at the step that removes it.

The zero-twist consecutive-root equations of Lemma <a href="#lem:bethe-data" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:bethe-data">3</a> have distinct real solutions with the following properties.*

1.  *If $\lambda>\pi/2$, the solutions at half filling exist for every finite homogeneous or real-array chain. Their counting derivative $\rho_L=\xi'$ is positive. If $$p_L(u)=\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}r(u-b_j),$$ then $\rho_L-p_L$ and their integrated discrepancy are uniformly bounded on the entire line.*



2.  *In the homogeneous chain, let $$r_Q=(I+K_Q)^{-1}s,\qquad H_Q=(I+K_Q)^{-1}1,$$ where $K_Q$ integrates over $[-Q,Q]$. For all $Q\ge0$, $$\label{eq:regular-density-comparison}
     c r(u)\le r_Q(u)\le C r(u),\qquad
     c\le H_Q(u)\le C\quad (|u|\le Q).$$ The mass $\int_{-Q}^Q r_Q$ increases to $1/2$, and $$\frac12-\int_{-Q}^Q r_Q\asymp e^{-Q/\eta}.$$ Choose $Q=Q_L$ by $\int_{-Q}^Q r_Q=n/L$. If $Q$ stays bounded with a positive limiting filling, or if $Lr(Q)$ tends to infinity at least as a fixed positive power of $L$, the roots exist for all sufficiently large $L$. If $y_j^0$ are the mid-quantiles of $Lr_Q$, then $Lr_Q(y_j^0)|y_j-y_j^0|\le C$ uniformly in $j$, and $$\label{eq:regular-counting-density}
     \xi'(u)=Lr_Q(u)+O(1)$$ on the interval, including a bounded counting extension at its endpoints.*


3.  *In all three limiting procedures the measures $$\nu_L=\sum_j\ell_j\delta_{y_j},\qquad
     \ell_j=\xi'(y_j)^{-1},$$ have uniformly bounded mass on intervals of length one. In a region whose slightly enlarged neighborhood has density at least $M$, their error against bounded Lipschitz tests on a fixed bounded interval is at most $$\label{eq:local-quadrature}
     \frac{C}{M}\bigl(\|\phi\|_\infty+\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(\phi)\bigr).$$ Thus $\nu_L$ converges locally to Lebesgue measure in the dense portions of the limits.*

*At zero twist the homogeneous vectors constructed from these roots are the sector Perron vectors. The Gaudin matrices are nonsingular, so each constructed finite root list has a real-analytic twist branch near zero.

In the limits specified above, $$\label{eq:Gaudin-sup-inverse}
 \|(I+K\ell)^{-1}\|_{\infty\to\infty}\le C,\qquad
 (K\ell)_{ij}=K(y_i-y_j)\ell_j.$$ There is also a small $a>0$ for which the inverse has uniformly bounded row norm after conjugation by either $e^{ay_i}$ or $e^{-ay_i}$.*

*Let $\chi_-,\chi_0,\chi_+$ be a nonnegative partition of unity whose two transitions are translates of fixed smooth profiles with bounded first derivatives. The middle function has compact support, the transitions lie in regions of a fixed sufficiently high density, and the two transition regions recede from one another. The left function is one to the left of its transition and zero to its right; the right function has the opposite convention at the right transition. Put $$D_L=\nu_L-\,\mathrm du,\qquad D^\bullet=\chi_\bullet D_L.$$ The mixed operator $$\label{eq:mixed-operator}
 I+R_0*(D^0+D^+)$$ has a uniform lower bound in the pointwise sup norm. The analogous assertion holds with the two ends interchanged.

There is a real harmonic function $U$ on $\mathcal S_\lambda$ such that, for every finite real-inhomogeneity chain, $$\label{eq:harmonic-row-bound}
 \|T^D(x)\|\le2\|D\|
                 \exp\left(\sum_jU(x-b_j)\right).$$ Choose a nonvanishing analytic function $\mathcal N_L$ whose modulus is the exponential on the right. In the homogeneous plane and array limits, the untwisted vacuum eigenvalue $\tau_L$ satisfies $$\label{eq:vacuum-modulus-normalization}
 |\tau_L(x)/\mathcal N_L(x)|\longrightarrow1$$ locally uniformly in the strip, with the moving-bulk convention <a href="#eq:array-limit-convention" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:array-limit-convention">[eq:array-limit-convention]</a> in the array case. Consequently, for each compact $\mathcal K\subset\mathcal S_\lambda$, $$\label{eq:normalized-row-bound}
 \limsup\sup_{x\in\mathcal K}\|A_L^D(x)\|\le2\|D\|.$$ In particular the vacuum denominator has no zeros on such a compact for all sufficiently large systems in the indicated sense.

Let $E=\mathbb C^m$ be the coefficient space of the fixed column list. For each admissible $\beta>0$ there is a subspace $F_{\beta,L}\subset E$ of codimension at most $r_\beta$ such that $$\label{eq:weighted-node-decay}
 |W(c)(u)|\le C_\beta e^{-\beta u}\|c\|_2
 \quad\hbox{at every positive root }u,\quad c\in F_{\beta,L}.$$ For a fixed finite list of increasing shifts, choose one discrepancy partition for the whole list. The resulting subspaces may be chosen nested. There are analogous flags at the negative end. The constants are uniform in the three limiting procedures, with regular filling understood as $Q\to\infty$ after $L\to\infty$. Restriction to any column coordinate subspace preserves the codimension bound and all unit-vector estimates.

On $\mathcal H$ the rows act as bounded holomorphic operator families, with $$\|A^D(z)\|\le 2\|D\|,\qquad
 A^D(z)^*=A^{X\overline D X}(-\overline z).$$ They satisfy the finite RTT exchange identities wherever their auxiliary intertwiner is invertible. There is a strongly continuous unitary group $U_b$, $b\in\mathbb R$, fixing $\Omega$, such that $$\label{eq:angular-covariance}
 U_bA^D(z)U_b^*=A^D(z+ib).$$ Words with real arguments in any nonempty open subinterval of $(-v,v)$ span a dense subspace. The family $P=A^I$ consists of commuting normal operators and has a simultaneous representation by almost surely holomorphic scalar functions $f$ on the strip, with $$\label{eq:angular-scalar-star}
 f(-\overline z)=\overline{f(z)},\qquad |f(z)|\le2.$$ In the half-plane coordinate this reality condition reads $f(\overline w)=\overline{f(w)}$.

There is $r_\lambda>0$, independent of the even chain length, for which the two homogeneous quotients $$T^D(z)T^I(z)^{-1},\qquad T^I(z)^{-1}T^D(z)$$ are holomorphic and bounded by $C_\lambda\|D\|$ on discs of radius $r_\lambda$ about $z=v$ and $z=-v$. The limiting rows have strong boundary values $$\label{eq:angular-endpoints}
 A^D(v)=SD_0,\qquad
 A^D(-v)=(XD^tX)_0S^{-1},$$ where $S$ is the unitary site shift and $S\Omega=\Omega$. Almost every spectral function extends analytically and without zeros to bands of a common positive width about $\operatorname{Re}z=\pm v$, has modulus one on these lines, and satisfies $|f|\le1$ between them. Consequently it is inner in the right half-plane, and every zero $\zeta$ satisfies $$\label{eq:angular-cone}
 \operatorname{Re}\zeta\ge c_0|\zeta|$$ for a fixed $c_0>0$. At each specified strict argument the spectral set on which $f$ vanishes has measure zero.

On $\mathcal D$, almost every spectral function is either $1$ or a finite Blaschke product in the right half-plane, with $$\label{eq:finite-inner-symmetry}
 f(0)=f(\infty)=1,\qquad
 f(\overline w)=\overline{f(w)}.$$ Its zeros obey <a href="#eq:angular-cone" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:angular-cone">[eq:angular-cone]</a>. In particular its degree is even; write it as $2n_f$ when it is nonconstant. Every zero produces a fixed-width, fixed-depth dip of $|f(e^s)|$ near its logarithmic radius.*





*There is a possibly infinite positive measure $\mu$ on the nonconstant functions and a measurable spectral vector $\mathsf h$ such that $$\label{eq:current-cocycle}
 J(w)\Omega=(1-f(w))\mathsf h,
 \qquad \mu(\,\mathrm df)=\|\mathsf h(f)\|^2\,\sigma(\,\mathrm df).$$ Here $\sigma$ is any scalar measure used for the direct-integral representation; the resulting measure $\mu$ does not depend on that choice. It is invariant under dilation of the argument. On the cross-section where the smallest zero radius is one, it has the form $$\label{eq:orbit-disintegration}
 \mu=\,\mathrm ds\,\nu(\,\mathrm dF),\qquad f(w)=F(e^s w),\qquad
 \kappa:=\nu(1)<\infty.$$ If $\mathcal L(F)$ is the difference between its largest and smallest logarithmic zero radii, then $$\label{eq:orbit-width-tail}
 \nu\{\mathcal L>t\}\le Ce^{-2\eta t}\quad(t\ge1).$$ For a fixed diagonal word $h$, $$\label{eq:diagonal-boost-tail}
 \|(1-P(e^s))h\|\le C_h e^{-\eta|s|}.$$ For products of fixed words placed at scales $r_i$, the corresponding bound outside their fixed internal argument ranges is $C\sum_i e^{-\eta|s-\log r_i|}$, uniformly when these ranges separate.

The nonnegative self-adjoint spectral operator $$\label{eq:angular-endpoint-energy}
 E=S^{-1}\partial_zP(z)\big|_{z=v},\qquad
 E(f)=-\left.\partial_s\arg f(ie^s)\right|_{s=0},$$ agrees on local-spin vectors with the vacuum-subtracted nearest-neighbor Hamiltonian whose link is $$\label{eq:angular-local-link}
 h_{j,j+1}=-\eta\,\operatorname{swap}_{j,j+1}R'_{j,j+1}(0)
 =\begin{pmatrix}
 \eta\cot\lambda&0&0&0\\
 0&0&-\eta/\sin\lambda&0\\
 0&-\eta/\sin\lambda&0&0\\
 0&0&0&\eta\cot\lambda
 \end{pmatrix}_{j,j+1}.$$ In particular $[h_{j,j+1},Z_j+Z_{j+1}]=0$ and $E\Omega=0$. The closure of the local-spin subspace reduces $E$, and its real-time evolution is the local Hamiltonian evolution. For all sufficiently small $u$, $$\label{eq:angular-E-H-comparison}
 \mathbf 1_{\{E\le u\}}\le\mathbf 1_{\{H_0\le Cu\}}.$$ With $Z_1=S^{-1}Z_0S$, the exact adjacent-pair identity is $$\label{eq:angular-adjacent-current}
 N(i)=\tfrac12 S^2(Z_0+Z_1).$$

Let $p$ denote the phase of the unit shift $S=P(i)$, modulo $2\pi$, and let $\operatorname{sym}$ average a measure at $p$ and $-p$. Set $$D_0=-\langle\Omega,[Z_0,[Z_0,h_{0,1}]]\Omega\rangle.$$ Away from $p=0$, the projection of the current measure obeys $$\label{eq:angular-sum-rule}
 2\operatorname{sym}\bigl((p)_*(E\mu)\bigr)
       =D_0\frac{\,\mathrm dp}{2\pi}.$$ On each orbit of degree $2n_F$, the phase winds monotonically through $n_F$ complete turns. In particular $$\label{eq:angular-degree-integral}
 \int n_F\,\mathrm d\nu(F)<\infty.$$

After an axial symmetry, any nonzero lattice displacement may be written $(k,j)$ with $k\ge1$ and $|j|\le k$. Its height variance is $$\label{eq:angular-variogram}
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_c\bigl(h(k,j)-h(0,0)\bigr)^2
 &=2\operatorname{Re}\int\bigl(1-f(1)^k f(\pm i)^{|j|}\bigr)\,\mathrm d\mu(f)\\
 &=4\kappa\log k+O(1),
 \end{aligned}$$ where the sign of $i$ is chosen according to the vertical direction. The error is uniform over these displacements. In particular the variance is bounded by $C(1+\log(1+|k|+|j|))$.

The iterated homogeneous stiffness satisfies $$\label{eq:stiffness-value}
 \lim_{Q\to\infty}\lim_{L\to\infty}L e_L''(0)
      =\frac2{\pi-\lambda}.$$

There are consistent skew-adjoint forms $j_\pm$ on $\mathcal D_\pm^{\mathrm{fin}}$ such that, for every $R>0$, $$\label{eq:chiral-form-bound}
 \|E_\pm(R)j_\pm E_\pm(R)\|\le CR,
 \qquad E_\pm(R)j_\pm\Omega=E_\pm(R)a_\pm\mathsf h.$$ Their vacuum means vanish. For $s>0$, the damped forms extend to bounded operators and satisfy $$\label{eq:chiral-damped-form}
                     \|e^{-sa_\pm}j_\pm e^{-sa_\pm}\|\le C/s.$$ For every nonnegative measurable function $g$ on $(0,\infty)$, $$\label{eq:chiral-Haar-integral}
             \int g(a_\pm(f))\,\mathrm d\mu(f)
                     =\kappa\int_0^\infty g(t)\frac{dt}{t}.$$

Take $r_1=\delta^{-1}$ and $r_2=\delta$, with $\delta\downarrow0$ through arbitrary positive real values. On core representatives, the joint spectral functions $(f(\delta^{-1}\,\cdot),f(\delta\,\cdot))$ converge in law in the compact-open topology of the right half-plane to the two tensor-factor functions $(F_1,F_2)$. Jointly with these functions, $$\label{eq:chiral-scaled-spectrum}
\begin{split}
 \delta^{-1}H_0&\longrightarrow A:=a_+\otimes I+I\otimes a_-,\\
 \delta^{-1}\arg S&\longrightarrow -a_+\otimes I+I\otimes a_-,
       \qquad \mathop{\mathrm{sgn}}P(1)\longrightarrow I
\end{split}$$ in spectral law. The argument is the principal argument near zero. There is an unbounded set of $R>0$ for which $$\label{eq:chiral-band-limit}
 Q_\delta(R):=\mathbf 1_{H_0/\delta\le R}
                       \longrightarrow Q(R):=\mathbf 1_{A\le R}$$ strongly on representatives. Bounded spectral multipliers obtained jointly from these limits have the same strong interpretation whenever their discontinuity sets have zero limiting state mass.*


*If $m_\delta\ge0$ and $l_\delta$ are integers with $\delta m_\delta\to x\ge0$ and $\delta l_\delta\to y$, then $$\label{eq:chiral-semigroup-limit}
 P(1)^{m_\delta}S^{l_\delta}\longrightarrow
       e^{-(x+iy)a_+}\otimes e^{-(x-iy)a_-}$$ strongly on representatives.

For $a=a_+$ and $j=j_+$, the bounded operators $$\label{eq:chiral-collision-operator}
 K_\epsilon=e^{-\epsilon a}(\epsilon j)e^{-\epsilon a}
                      (\epsilon j)e^{-\epsilon a}$$ are uniformly bounded for $\epsilon>0$ and satisfy $$\label{eq:chiral-collision-limit}
                    K_\epsilon\rightharpoonup-\kappa I
                       \qquad(\epsilon\downarrow0).$$ Consequently, fix $z_2,\ldots,z_k$ with pairwise distinct real coordinates and pairwise distinct imaginary coordinates, and let $2\le j\le k$. Then $$\label{eq:chiral-double-pole}
 \lim_{\epsilon\downarrow0}\epsilon^2
 F_k(z_j+\epsilon,z_2,\ldots,z_k)
   =-\kappa F_{k-2}(z_2,\ldots,\widehat z_j,\ldots,z_k).$$ The coefficient on the right is independent of both colliding positions.

Let $k\ge1$, and let $z_1,\ldots,z_k$ have pairwise distinct real coordinates or pairwise distinct imaginary coordinates. For $\varepsilon_i\in\{+,-\}$ and moving mesh faces $z_{i,\delta}\to z_i$, $$\label{eq:chiral-Gaussian-current-limit}
 \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \mathbb E\prod_{i=1}^k
       \delta^{-1}D_\delta^{\varepsilon_i}h_\delta(z_{i,\delta})
   =\sum_{\pi}\ \prod_{\{i,j\}\in\pi}
                  C_{\varepsilon_i,\varepsilon_j}(z_i,z_j),$$ where the sum is over pairings, is zero for odd $k$, and $$\label{eq:chiral-contractions}
 C_{++}(z,\xi)=-\frac{\kappa}{(z-\xi)^2},\qquad
 C_{--}(z,\xi)=-\frac{\kappa}{(\bar z-\bar\xi)^2},\qquad
 C_{+-}(z,\xi)=C_{-+}(z,\xi)=0.$$ Convergence is uniform on compact subsets of either separated-time region. By linearity the corresponding assertion holds for all products of the real centered derivatives $\delta^{-1}D_{x,\delta}$ and $\delta^{-1}D_{y,\delta}$. These are the off-diagonal derivatives of the Gaussian covariance $$\label{eq:chiral-log-covariance}
                              K_\kappa(z,\xi)=-2\kappa\log|z-\xi|.$$

Fix $0<A<\infty$. Let $R\ge1$, and let $g=(g_l)_{l\in\mathbb Z}$ be a finitely supported real sequence such that $$\label{eq:prob-g-conditions}
 \sum_l|g_l|\le AR,\qquad
 \sum_l|g_{l+1}-g_l|\le A,\qquad
 \sum_l|g_{l+1}-g_l|^2\le A/R.$$ Then, for every $1\le b<\infty$, $$\label{eq:prob-ordinary-moments}
 \left\|\sum_lg_lZ_l\right\|_{L^b(\mathbb P_c)}\le C_{A,b}.$$ In particular the conclusion holds if $g$ has support of length at most $AR$, $|g_l|\le A$, and $|g_{l+1}-g_l|\le A/R$. The same assertions hold with the two axes interchanged.

Let $0<\ell\le1$, and let $\psi_{b,\ell}$ be supported in a square of side $A\ell$ about $b$, with $|\psi_{b,\ell}|\le A\ell^{-2}$. For centers $b$ in a fixed compact set and every finite $q$, $$\label{eq:prob-pinned-averages}
 \|\langle X_\delta^\chi,\psi_{b,\ell}\rangle\|_{L^q}
 \le C_{A,q,\chi,\rho}(1+|\log\ell|).$$ The estimate is uniform in $\delta>0$.

Let $Q$ be a square of side $0<\ell\le1$ in a fixed bounded box, and let $A\subset Q$ be measurable with $|A|>0$. Put $t=|A|/\ell^2$. Then for every finite $b$, $$\label{eq:prob-cell-average}
 \left\|\frac1{|A|}\int_A X_\delta(x)\,dx\right\|_{L^b}
 \le C_b(1+|\log\ell|+|\log t|)$$ uniformly in $\delta$ and in the shape of $A$.

For every even $k\ge2$ and every compact $K\subset\mathcal D_k$, $$\lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \sup_{\boldsymbol u\in K}\sup_{I\in\operatorname{Isom}(\mathbb R^2)}
 \left|\Phi_{k,\delta}(\boldsymbol u)
             -\Phi_{k,\delta}(I\boldsymbol u)\right|=0.
 \label{eq:rr-target}$$

Let $N(a,M)$ be the number of loops of diameter at least $a$ meeting $B(0,M)$. For $\delta$ sufficiently smaller than $a$, and every fixed $p<\infty$, $$\|N(a,M)\|_{L^p}\le C_p(1+M/a)^2.
 \label{eq:rr-count}$$ There are $C,\gamma>0$ such that, with harmless fixed changes of the radii, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P\{\exists L:L\cap B(0,M)\ne\varnothing,
                            L\not\subset B(0,R)\}
      &\le C(M/R)^\gamma, && R\ge10M,
       \label{eq:rr-escape}\\
 \mathbb P\{\exists L:\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}L\ge a,
                       \mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(z,L)\le r\}
      &\le C\bigl((r+C\delta)/a\bigr)^\gamma,
          &&0<r<a/100.
       \label{eq:rr-point}
\end{aligned}$$ The constants are independent of the deterministic center $z$.
